description:
Motivation: many production workloads see intermittent latency spikes and preventable token cost from cold KV/cache priming and suboptimal batching presets. This PR introduces a new lightweight service (cache-pacer) that maintains speculative cache warmup for hot prefixes and pairs it with a cost-aware preset generator that recommends batching/caching/quantization profiles per workload segment. Goals: reduce tail latency due to cold KV misses, cut per-token cost by recommending safer batch/cache/quant profiles, and provide an operator-friendly UI to preview and apply presets. Summary of changes: 1) runtime: speculative cache pacer that watches per-route token-access heatmaps and issues non-blocking prefetches; integrates with KV cache manager and honors memory budgets and SLO signals; 2) optimizer: cost-presets generator simulates unit economics for candidate presets using sampled traces and returns ranked list with estimated latency/cost deltas; 3) console: presets preview/editor and rollout canary control; 4) infra: helm value to toggle feature and set budgets; 5) tests/workloads: new load tests and a microbench harness for unit-econ sim. Checklist: - [x] Design doc + perf model (ENG-6142) - [x] Spec for pacer backpressure and budget enforcement - [x] Implementation in runtime and optimizer - [x] Console UI for preview/apply - [x] End-to-end smoke and canary tests - [x] Added Linear tickets for rollout monitoring (ENG-6190) How it works (brief): The cache-pacer subscribes to aggregated heatmaps emitted by the serving runtime (prefix frequency and KV miss rates). It computes a prioritized prefetch queue with a soft budget B (tokens memory footprint) and an urgency score that factors SLO headroom and recent miss latency. Prefetches are scheduled at low priority; if the runtime signals memory pressure or the SLO headroom drops, in-flight prefetches are aborted. The cost-preset generator takes a sampled trace of requests (seq length distribution + target latency SLO) and evaluates candidate presets (batch size, prefix cache toggle, qprofile) via a fast cost model (tokenized throughput, expected cache hit uplift, quantization speedup). The generator outputs delta estimates and a safety score. Rollout plan: presets can be previewed in console and applied as canaries by route percentage. Feature is gated by helm flag (cachePacer.enabled=false by default) and a runtime feature flag. Commits: 1) add speculative pacer core and interfaces 2) add cost-preset generator and CLI --simulate-presets 3) console: presets editor + rollout controls 4) tests and workload harness 5) docs and helm toggle Notable decisions: - Prefetches are non-blocking and use best-effort KV warmup only; never blocks request serving - Default generator candidate space is intentionally conservative: batch sizes limited to current SLO-derived ceiling; qprofiles only from vetted set - Safety score penalizes changes that reduce cache use for routes with >70% cache hit dependence Observability and metrics added: pacer.queue_length, pacer.prefetch_success, pacer.prefetch_aborts, preset_sim.estimated_cost_delta, preset_sim.latency_pct_change, preset_sim.safety_score Files changed (high level): - runtime/pacer/cache_pacer.go: new pacer implementation and interfaces (added ~380 LOC) - runtime/pacer/pacer_test.go: unit tests for pacing and abort logic - optimizer/presets/simulator.py: fast unit-econ simulator for presets - optimizer/presets/generator.go: Go wrapper that schedules simulations and returns ranked presets - console/src/components/PresetsEditor.tsx: presets preview UI and canary controls - infra/helm/values.yaml: feature gate and default budgets - examples/workloads/microbench/trace_replay.py: synthetic trace replay for canary - tests/e2e/pacer_canary.yaml: e2e canary manifest Small illustrative snippet (runtime/pacer/cache_pacer.go): "// schedule best-effort prefetch; returns immediately if memoryBudget exhausted
func (p *Pacer) schedule(prefetch Prefetch) {
  if atomic.LoadInt64(&p.memUsed) + prefetch.estimate > p.memBudget {
    p.metrics.prefetch_aborts.Inc()
    return
  }
  p.queue.Push(prefetch)
}" Review conversation (summary as continuous text): Reviewer Jordan Liu: "This looks promising — nice separation between urgency scoring and budget enforcement. A few asks: document edge cases for cancellation and add a test for prefetch abort when mem pressure spikes." Author (Ava): "Added pacer_test.go covering abort and cancel paths; expanded docstring and inline comments. Also added a metric for prefetch_aborts so we can alert if it rises unexpectedly." Reviewer Priya Nair: "Simulator seems conservative; can we widen candidate qprofile set?" Author: "For safety we limited initial qprofiles to the validated set (q8_4, q4_0). We added a CLI flag --allow-experimental-q to run an extended sweep in staging only; not enabled in console by default." Reviewer Ethan Cole: "Can we surface a recommended confidence band in the UI?" Author: "Added estimated delta + safety_score and a textual recommendation (apply/preview/skip) in the UI; we will iterate after canary telemetry." CI checks and outcomes: - unit: runtime tests (pass) - integration: optimizer simulation (flaky on first run due to random trace seed) -> rerun -> pass - lint: ts/js/py (pass with minor autofixes) - e2e-canary: deploy manifest smoke -> pass After CI stabilized and reviewers approved, merged via squash-merge. Merge details: merged_by: "Priya Nair", merge_method: "squash", merge_commit_message: "Introduce cache-pacer and cost-preset generator; console preview and canary controls (ENG-6142)" Canary and rollout notes: - Feature gate off by default in helm; enable in staging for 1 week and monitor pacer.prefetch_aborts and preset_sim.estimated_cost_delta - Start with 5% route canary for presets then 25% then 100% if safety_score remains above 0.8 for 48h - Monitoring dashboards created: infra/observability/dashboards/cache_pacer.json Known limitations and follow-ups: - Current simulator uses sampled traces; full production sampling pipeline (higher fidelity) is tracked in ENG-6288 - Prefetching does not currently rate-limit by per-customer budgets (planned follow-up ENG-6261) - Longer-term: integrate with optimizer autoscaler to auto-tune memBudget per pool (ENG-6302)
